1. In the Rectangle class, on initialization, two assert statements will fail if the arguments 'l' and 'w' are not greater than 0.

```python
class Rectangle(Shape):
    def __init__(self, l, w):
        assert l > 0 and w > 0, "Invalid values passed in"
```

2. In the Circle class, on initialization, one assert statement will fail if the argument 'r' is not greater than 0.

```python
class Circle(Shape):
    def __init__(self, r):
        assert r > 0, "Invalid values passed in"
```

3. In the compare_a function, two assert statements can help check if both 's1' and 's2' are legitimate shapes (i.e., have valid area and perimeter values), before comparing their areas, as follows:

```python
def compare_a(s1, s2):
    assert (s1.area > 0 and s1.perimeter > 0) and (s2.area > 0 and s2.perimeter > 0), "Invalid shapes passed in"
```

All assert failures will raise an AssertionError exception.--------------------